          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The cluster primarily identifies the humble, lowly, and dependent faithful—the anawim—who rely on God, receive his deliverance, and inherit his kingdom, while often also being materially poor or socially powerless.\",\"book_summary\":\"The terms identify those bowed humbly and empty-handed before God, receptive to the gospel and heirs of the kingdom.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Humble, God-dependent people who receive divine favor and salvation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Humble, empty-handed people before God who receive the gospel and kingdom.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is explicit. The book foregrounds spiritual humility and receptivity without expressly excluding literal poverty or social need, which the consensus treats as overlapping senses.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book's identification corresponds directly to the consensus's primary anawim or humble-faithful reading.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum identification, both refer to the lowly and humble who depend on God and consequently receive his saving favor and kingdom. The book's language of being 'empty-handed' and able to receive is an interpretive restatement of spiritual dependence, not a changed referent or materially narrowed class.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Several cited passages, especially Psalm 72:12–13 and James 2:5, directly concern materially poor or socially helpless people, so the book may understate the socioeconomic dimension by concentrating on inward humility. That emphasis does not, however, amount to an explicit exclusion or a different core identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
